WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.920
Authorization failures happen when an application does not consistently verify whether an

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.380
authenticated user is allowed to access a record or perform an action.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.880
Authentication answers who the user is; authorization answers what that user may do.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.740
Privilege escalation can let a normal user perform administrative actions or access data

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:25.160
meant for another user, project, contract, or business unit. In CUI environments,

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.980
that can turn an application flaw into an information disclosure incident.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.880
Insecure direct object references occur when object identifiers such as record IDs,

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.180
file names, case numbers,

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.640
or document references are exposed without checking whether the current user is authorized

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.380
for the target object.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.880
Access control bypasses often appear in hidden routes, disabled client-side controls,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.900
unprotected APIs, and forced browsing.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.760
The server must enforce access decisions because client-side behavior is not a security

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:01.780
boundary. Apply least privilege to users, services, and administrators.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.680
Enforce access decisions on the server for every sensitive action.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.460
Use deny-by-default design so access must be explicitly granted.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:18.040
Selected CMMC mappings for this topic include AC.L2-3.1.1 - Limit system access;

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:27.220
AC.L2-3.1.2 - Authorized users; AC.L2-3.1.5 - Least privilege.
